# TODO - Android ARM64 runtime and packaging

## Objective

Deliver the intended Android ARM64 target through the shared capability
IR without blocking implementation on already supported targets.

## Acceptance

- Install or discover a supported official Android SDK and NDK.
- Implement missing Android graphics, audio, lifecycle, input and storage
  adapters through the shared IR; preserve explicit capability absence.
- Build, link, package and verify an ARM64 application using the official
  toolchain; preserve the shared asset namespace and licence boundaries.
- Exercise startup, touch, storage, rendering and audio on an available
  Android device or emulator; record physical-device checks separately.
- Record unsupported capabilities and do not claim Android compatibility
  from portable compilation alone.

## Evidence

Blocked: on 2026-11-26 the Android Rust target and ADB were available,
but the SDK, NDK and cross-linker were absent. The completed mobile-web
record retains portable compilation evidence. The browser package does
not imply Android support. Continue title implementation on supported
targets while this target is unavailable; do not mark it complete.

On 2026-11-27 the prerequisite blocker was resolved with the official
Google Android command-line tools (23.0; the Linux archive matched the
published size and SHA-1). The user-shared SDK root is
`$HOME/.local/share/android-sdk`. Android Platform 35, Build-Tools
35.0.0, Platform-Tools 37.0.3, NDK r28c (28.2.13676358), Emulator
37.3.10 and the API 35 Google APIs x86-64 system image are installed.
The NDK identifies Clang 19.0.1 and its API 24 AArch64 target. A Rust
standalone PIE links with `aarch64-linux-android24-clang`; ELF inspection
confirms AArch64 and the Android `/system/bin/linker64` interpreter.

A disposable JNI probe links a Rust ARM64 shared library, compiles its
minimal Java activity against API 35, packages only `arm64-v8a`, aligns
its native payload and passes APK signature schemes v2 and v3. This
checks the official toolchain, not the game or its capability adapters.
No original assets or production signing credentials were used.

KVM acceleration passes. Two initial headless starts of a private API 35
AVD crashed before Android boot completion, including a retry with audio
disabled and SwiftShader selected. QEMU exited 139 (SIGSEGV, confirmed by
the system coredump inventory). ADB reported no connected device.

A separate private API 35 Google APIs x86-64 AVD with two CPU cores,
2 GiB RAM, software graphics, Vulkan disabled and no audio survived a
105-second cold start; ADB was offline when that bounded attempt ended.
The next start reached boot completion and reported API 35 and
`x86_64,arm64-v8a` ABIs with the `libndk_translation.so` native bridge.
The independently built ARM64-only JNI APK installed and cold-launched;
its UIAutomator hierarchy contained the integer 42 returned by Rust.
The emulator shut down cleanly. The working headless configuration used
`-gpu software -feature -Vulkan -cores 2 -memory 2048 -no-audio`,
no cameras and no snapshot. This proves execution through translation,
not native ARM64 hardware equivalence.

The release engine, title core and platform libraries also link into a
disposable ARM64-only JNI probe using the NDK API 24 target. The Rust
link uses thin LTO to consume the workspace's bitcode with Rust's LLVM
rather than the NDK's older LLVM reader. The existing core smoke runs
unchanged except for its private entry point; it exercises deterministic
frame partitioning, four-station movement, pause, logical touch
projection and save-envelope rejection. The probe also calls the shared
monotonic clock, native save slots and display preference stores in
dedicated app-private directories. Three cold starts reported 201, 202
and 203, verifying first publication, replacement, slot isolation,
oversized-payload rejection, successful durability receipts and
preference reload across separate processes. No original media was used.

The remaining work is the Android application lifecycle, graphics,
audio, physical input and storage-location integration, and the game
APK. Core execution and app-private persistence are established only
through translated emulation, not a playable title or native ARM64
hardware acceptance.

The pinned SDL2 0.39.0 Rust dependency's upstream native source builds
with NDK r28c for AArch64 at API 24. A disposable APK contains only the
ARM64 libraries and upstream SDL Activity and diagnostic entry points.
It passes alignment and APK v2/v3 signature verification. On the
translated API 35 emulator, the native diagnostic selects OpenGL ES 2,
observes rotation and the new 1920 by 1080 extent, receives native
finger down/up and synthesized mouse events, and observes window focus
loss and recovery across Home and relaunch. The first run also observed
surface destruction and native pause/resume. No title assets were
included.

SDL's filesystem diagnostic returns the app-private files directory
from its preference-path API; the executable base-path query is
unsupported. SDL selects OpenSL ES and enumerates one output and one
capture device, but default-device format discovery is unsupported.
The headless emulator ran with audio disabled; no audible playback or
real controller behavior has been verified.

The shared input contract now distinguishes application suspension from
window focus loss. SDL background notifications release all held
physical sources without detaching controllers, cancel active control
editor drafts, invalidate touch geometry and stop feedback. Termination
maps to quit. Application foreground alone does not grant window focus.
Unit and all-feature validation pass on the development host; this is
not verification of the Android title adapter or suspended simulation
timing.

The pinned Rust-SDL2 wrapper does not supply an Android application
entry point. Its Java Activity invokes the native SDL main symbol.
An owned unsafe exported entry or a shim bypassing the workspace
unsafe-code prohibition is not an acceptable solution. A supported
safe entry wrapper or other lawful platform integration is required.
The upstream diagnostics establish dependency feasibility only; the
Android target remains explicitly unavailable for interactive titles.

Two safe-entry alternatives were investigated in disposable Rust 2024
ARM64 shared-library probes with `unsafe_code = "forbid"` and warnings
as errors. `safer-ffi` 0.2.0-rc1 exports `SDL_main` under the NDK linker,
but its resolved dependencies introduce both syn 1 and syn 2, plus
multiple extension-trait crate versions. The stable 0.1.13 candidate
also depends on syn 1. Neither was admitted to the workspace.
SDL3 0.22.1 builds its upstream Android library with NDK r28c, but
`sdl3-main` 0.7.0 generates a Rust executable main rather than an exported
Android `SDL_main` in the shared-library probe. An alternate JNI entry
using the SDL3 Activity's overridable Java main and `jni` 0.22.4 fails
strict compilation because its native method macro emits a deprecated
atomic API; its dependency resolution also introduces syn 3 alongside
syn 2. No candidate was added to the workspace, no diagnostic APK was
published, and no Android title runtime acceptance is implied.

A separate disposable probe uses the documented SDL3 Activity `main`
override and the JNI 0.22.4 `jni_mangle` attribute with a typed
`EnvUnowned` and panic-catching `with_env` boundary. The Rust 2024
ARM64 library links with unsafe code forbidden and warnings denied,
without the deprecated native-method macro or an owned native shim.
Its isolated dependency resolution uses thiserror 2.0.18 and retains
one syn major version; this is not workspace dependency admission.
The ARM64-only APK verifies alignment and v2/v3 signatures.

On the API 35 x86-64 emulator with ARM64 translation, a cold launch
enters the Rust SDL3 event loop. Center-pixel readback changes from
RGBA (40, 80, 180, 255) to (12, 180, 40, 255) after a touch, then
retains the latter color after Home and foreground restoration. The
Activity observes focus loss and recovery and logs the normal Rust
return value 42. The isolated emulator shuts down. No title media,
production credentials or installed application state were used.

This establishes a candidate safe entry route with SDL3, not the
existing SDL2 integration. The development host lacks a system SDL3
package; the existing Linux graphics, input and audio adapters must
not be duplicated or regressed. Shared adapter migration, workspace
feature and dependency checks, application storage and asset paths,
actual title packaging, audio and physical ARM64 acceptance remain
open. No workspace dependency or owner configuration was changed.

A disposable Linux compatibility probe builds SDL3 0.22.1 with its
upstream SDL 3.4.20 source statically linked, without installing a
system development package. X11, offscreen and a private headless
Wayland compositor with a virtual monitor select OpenGL and verify
exact RGBA nearest-neighbor texture readback. The default headless
Wayland session has no displays. An injected fractional mouse event
retains its coordinates; SDL3 event timestamps are nanoseconds rather
than the existing SDL2 millisecond timestamps.

Dummy audio verifies default and enumerated device selection, exact
48 kHz stereo signed-16 source and destination, a 1024-frame obtained
period, byte-counted queue admission, clearing, resuming, draining
and pausing. This is not audible playback or validation of differing
physical device formats. The shared audio IR now admits fallible
backend observations and retains terminal control results until
their state and queue receipts can be verified; regression tests
exercise synchronous, asynchronous and rejected controls. Linux and
browser adapters retain their existing dependencies.

The SDL3 wrapper's rumble and sensor capability queries require
unsafe calls. A separate strict compile probe rejects both; existing
Linux capability discovery must not be degraded to migrate. Another
SDL2 entry candidate, interoptopus 0.16.6, emits an unsafe trait
implementation and export attribute in the strict ARM64 probe and is
not admitted. Neither probe modifies workspace dependencies. Native
adapter migration remains blocked on a compatible safe integration,
and Android title packaging and physical-device acceptance remain open.

A separate SDL2 safe-entry proof removes the need for a wholesale
SDL3 migration. Rust SDL2 0.39.0 with its pinned SDL 2.32.10 native
source and JNI 0.22.4 link as an ARM64-only shared library with unsafe
Rust forbidden and warnings denied. A private copy of SDLActivity
delegates its existing SDLMain runner through one Java override;
the probe's override calls the JNI-mangled Rust method. The Rust
worker is created by Rust, letting SDL own its JNI attachment and
detachment. There is no owned native shim or unsafe export attribute.
The APK passes alignment and v2/v3 signature verification.

On the translated API 35 emulator, a cold launch opens accelerated
SDL2 rendering. Center pixels change from (40, 80, 180, 255) to
(12, 180, 40, 255) after a touch and remain green after Home and
foreground restoration. The Activity logs focus loss and recovery
and the worker returns 42 normally. This result also requires a
nonempty SDL audio driver name and an app-private preference path.
The emulator shuts down. The isolated probe does not exercise
shared IR rendering, the audio queue or a playable title. It uses
no original media or production signing credentials.

The existing shared SDL2 graphics, input and PCM implementations now
select on Android with the interactive feature. Linux alone enables
host pkg-config discovery. The mandatory validation gate includes
strict Clippy of the interactive Android ARM64 platform library in
addition to the portable check. The fresh gate and pre-commit hook
passed for the shared adapter change.

An ARM64-only diagnostic linked the actual shared platform IR and
passed APK alignment and v2/v3 verification. On the translated API 35
emulator it opened an accelerated scene, uploaded a bounded one-pixel
atlas and checked the complete 320 by 240 RGBA readback after admitting
a touch against presented geometry. It observed focus loss or suspension
and retained the rendered scene after foreground restoration. Shared
NativeAudio accepted a 48 kHz signed-16 stereo queue, retained 1024
paused frames, resumed and drained to zero, then paused and cleared.
All checks were required for return value 42. The host emulator ran
with audio disabled, so this is not audible playback acceptance.
No game was packaged and no original media was used.

A disposable split-crate entry probe also retains a platform-owned JNI
export in a title shared library under thin LTO, one codegen unit and
stripped debug information. Only its platform crate depends on JNI
0.22.4 and inventory 0.3.25; the title registers a typed Rust callback.
Both crates forbid unsafe Rust and deny warnings. The isolated lock
uses thiserror 2.0.18 to retain one syn major in the Android graph.
Two signed ARM64-only diagnostic APKs launched on the translated API 35
emulator: exactly one registered callback returned 42; two registered
callbacks returned -1 without invoking the callback. No dependency
or launcher from this probe has been admitted to the workspace.

Actual title launcher composition, process output and app-private asset
and settings paths, physical ARM64, sensors, controller feedback and
production packaging remain open. Interactive adapter selection alone
is not a claim of playable Android title compatibility.
